    Overflow test?

    (Was testing the b*srch2 functions but found something
    else instead....)

    Basically, when you start with a range where the
    difference is promoted to an NV, the bitwise shift
    doesn't actually divide by 2.  For instance, if we
    take [-1, uvmax], the midpoint should be 0x80{15},
    but shifting gives 0x7f{15}:

    $ perl -e 'printf "%a\n", ($_ = ~0 + 1);
       printf "%#x, %#x\n", ($_ / 2), ($_ >> 1);'
    0x1p+64
    0x8000000000000000, 0x7fffffffffffffff

    This causes the first index to be off-by-one:

    $ perl -MSort::Search=bisectl -e 'bisectl
     { die sprintf "%#x\n", $_[0] } -1, ~0'
    0x7ffffffffffffffe

    unless something that doesn't overflow is used:

    $ perl -MSort::Search=bisectl -MMath::BigInt -e 'bisectl
     { die sprintf "%#x\n", $_[0] } -1, Math::BigInt->new(~0)'
    0x7fffffffffffffff

    In fact, the bit shift seems to always (?) be -1,
    even if they are actually quite far apart:

    $ perl -e 'printf "%a\n", ($_ = ~0 - (-1 - (~0 >> 1)));
       printf "%#x, %#x\n", ($_ / 2), ($_ >> 1);'
    0x1.8p+64
    0xc000000000000000, 0x7fffffffffffffff

    So this is not the best thing... we either warn people
    about this in CAVEATS, *or* -- I'm thinking -- maybe we
    could compute the first midpoint differently; then as
    we shrink we should never get promoted to an NV (the
    largest conceivable range is [ivmin, uvmax] above gets
    shrunk down to 0xbf{15}, which fits in a UV! :)

